  {
    "id": "langchain-langgraph",
    "title": "LangChain and LangGraph: Frameworks, and When to Use One",
    "subtitle_en": "The Log Pose points at one island; the New World needs a chart you drew yourself",
    "subtitle_ne": "Log Pose ले एउटै टापु देखाउँछ; New World लाई तिमी आफैंले कोरेको chart चाहिन्छ",
    "intro_en": "what an abstraction buys and what it costs, why linear chains stop being enough, the graph model that replaced them, and an honest rule for when to adopt a framework at all",
    "intro_ne": "अमूर्तताले के किन्छ र के खर्च गराउँछ, रैखिक chain किन पर्याप्त हुन छाड्छ, तिनलाई विस्थापित गर्ने graph model, र framework कहिले अपनाउने भन्ने इमानदार नियम",
    "slides": [
      {
        "heading": "What a framework gives you, and what it takes",
        "bullets": [
          "The real value is the boring surface area: loaders for forty document formats, splitters, a uniform interface across model providers, vector-store adapters, streaming, callbacks and tracing. <b>Weeks of plumbing you did not write.</b>",
          "The cost is indirection. When something misbehaves you are debugging <i>their</i> control flow, and the prompt actually sent is three classes away from the code you wrote.",
          "<b>The rule that resolves it: learn the mechanics first, adopt the abstraction second.</b> You have now built RAG and an agent loop by hand, so a framework is a labour saver rather than a black box.",
          "And the surrounding tooling is often the stronger argument. <b>LangSmith-style tracing</b> — seeing every prompt, tool call and token cost in one timeline — is genuinely hard to build yourself and is what teams end up keeping."
        ],
        "narration_en": "Now that you have written a RAG pipeline and an agent loop by hand, we can have the framework conversation honestly, which is difficult to do in the other order. Start with what a framework actually gives you, because the useful part is not the part that gets advertised. It is the boring surface area. Document loaders for forty different formats, so you are not writing a PDF parser this afternoon. Text splitters that respect structure. A uniform interface across model providers so switching is a config change — which, as we said last part, is something you would have built anyway. Adapters for a dozen vector stores. Streaming, retries, callbacks, token accounting, tracing. Individually each of these is a small piece of work; collectively they are several weeks, and none of them is the interesting part of your product. That is a real offer and it deserves to be taken seriously. Now the cost, which is indirection, and it is also real. When a chain misbehaves, you are no longer debugging your own control flow — you are debugging somebody else's, through documentation, and the prompt that was actually sent to the model is assembled three classes away from anything you wrote. Frameworks in this space have also moved quickly and broken things, so code written a year ago may not run today. And there is a subtler cost: an abstraction that hides the mechanism will also hide your understanding of it, which is fine until the day you need to reason about why something is expensive or slow or wrong. The rule that resolves this cleanly is about ordering, not about whether frameworks are good. Learn the mechanics first, adopt the abstraction second. You have done that. You know what a chunk is, what an embedding is, what the prompt looks like, and what the agent loop does, because you wrote them. So when a framework does those things for you, it is saving you labour rather than hiding a mystery, and when it does something surprising you can open it up and read it, because you know what it is supposed to be doing. Somebody who started with the framework has no such recourse. And one thing worth flagging because teams discover it late: the strongest argument for adopting a framework is often the observability rather than the abstractions. A tracing tool that shows you every prompt, every tool call, every intermediate result and the token cost of each, laid out in one timeline for a single request, is genuinely hard to build yourself and enormously useful once your system has more than two steps in it. Several teams end up using very little of a framework's chain machinery and all of its tracing, and that is a perfectly sensible outcome.",
        "narration_ne": "अब तिमीले RAG pipeline र agent loop हातले लेखिसकेपछि, हामी framework को कुरा इमानदारीपूर्वक गर्न सक्छौं, जुन अर्को क्रममा गर्न गाह्रो छ। Framework ले साँच्चै के दिन्छ त्यहाँबाट सुरु गर, किनभने उपयोगी भाग प्रचार हुने भाग होइन। त्यो नीरस सतह क्षेत्र हो। चालीस फरक ढाँचाका कागजात loader, ताकि तिमी आज दिउँसो PDF parser नलेख। संरचना मान्ने text splitter। model प्रदायकभरि एउटै interface ताकि साट्नु config को परिवर्तन होस् — जुन, अघिल्लो भागमा भनेजस्तै, तिमीले जे भए पनि बनाउने थियौ। दर्जनौं vector भण्डारका adapter। Streaming, retry, callback, token हिसाब, tracing। छुट्टाछुट्टै हरेक सानो काम हो; सामूहिक रूपमा ती कैयौं हप्ता हुन्, र तीमध्ये कुनै पनि तिम्रो उत्पादनको रोचक भाग होइन। त्यो साँचो प्रस्ताव हो र गम्भीरतापूर्वक लिन योग्य छ। अब लागत, जुन अप्रत्यक्षता हो, र त्यो पनि साँचो छ। chain ले गलत व्यवहार गर्दा, तिमी अब आफ्नै नियन्त्रण प्रवाह debug गर्दैनौ — तिमी अरू कसैको, कागजातमार्फत, debug गर्दैछौ, र model लाई साँच्चै पठाइएको prompt तिमीले लेखेको कुनै पनि कुराबाट तीन class पर जोडिन्छ। यस क्षेत्रका framework छिटो सरेका र कुरा भाँचेका पनि छन्, त्यसैले एक वर्षअघि लेखिएको code आज नचल्न सक्छ। र सूक्ष्म लागत छ: संयन्त्र लुकाउने अमूर्तताले तिम्रो बुझाइ पनि लुकाउँछ, जुन कुनै कुरा किन महँगो वा सुस्त वा गलत छ भनी तर्क गर्नुपर्ने दिनसम्म ठीकै हुन्छ। यसलाई सफा समाधान गर्ने नियम क्रमबारे हो, framework राम्रा हुन् कि होइनन् भन्नेबारे होइन। पहिले संयन्त्र सिक, दोस्रो अमूर्तता अपनाऊ। तिमीले त्यो गर्‍यौ। तिमीलाई टुक्रा के हो, embedding के हो, prompt कस्तो देखिन्छ, र agent loop ले के गर्छ थाहा छ, किनभने तिमीले ती लेख्यौ। त्यसैले framework ले ती काम तिम्रा लागि गर्दा, यसले रहस्य लुकाउनुको सट्टा तिम्रो श्रम बचाउँदैछ, र यसले अचम्मको काम गर्दा तिमी खोलेर पढ्न सक्छौ, किनभने यसले के गर्नुपर्ने हो तिमीलाई थाहा छ। framework बाट सुरु गर्नेसँग त्यस्तो उपाय छैन। र एउटा कुरा औंल्याउन लायक छ किनभने टोलीले ढिलो पत्ता लगाउँछन्: framework अपनाउने सबैभन्दा बलियो तर्क प्राय: अमूर्तता होइन, observability हुन्छ। एउटै अनुरोधका लागि हरेक prompt, हरेक tool call, हरेक बीचको नतिजा र हरेकको token लागत एउटै timeline मा देखाउने tracing उपकरण आफैं बनाउन साँच्चै गाह्रो छ र तिम्रो प्रणालीमा दुईभन्दा बढी पाइला भएपछि अत्यन्त उपयोगी छ। कैयौं टोलीले framework को chain संयन्त्र अत्यन्त थोरै र यसको tracing पूरै प्रयोग गर्छन्, र त्यो पूर्ण रूपमा उचित नतिजा हो।"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Chains, and where the straight line breaks",
        "bullets": [
          "LangChain's core idea is composition: <code>prompt | model | parser</code>, each piece a runnable, piped together. For a linear pipeline this is genuinely pleasant and removes real boilerplate.",
          "It works because most early LLM applications <i>were</i> linear: retrieve, stuff into a prompt, generate, parse. A chain is exactly the right shape for that.",
          "<b>Then the requirements arrive that a line cannot express.</b> Retry with a different strategy. Branch on a classification. Loop until a check passes. Pause for human approval and resume later.",
          "You can force those into a chain with callbacks and conditional runnables, and the result is a control-flow graph written sideways — <b>which is the argument for making the graph the primitive instead.</b>"
        ],
        "narration_en": "LangChain's central idea is composition, and it is a good idea. Everything is a runnable — a prompt template, a model, an output parser, a retriever — and runnables compose with a pipe operator. Prompt pipe model pipe parser. Read left to right, data flows through, and the whole thing has one interface so you can swap any piece. For a linear pipeline it is genuinely pleasant, and it removes a real amount of boilerplate around formatting, invoking, streaming and parsing. This design was a good fit for its moment, and the reason is worth noticing. Almost every early LLM application was linear. Take the user's question. Retrieve some documents. Stuff them into a prompt. Call the model. Parse the output. Return it. That is a straight line with five stations on it, and a chain is exactly the right abstraction for a straight line. Nobody was wrong to build it. Then applications got more ambitious, and the requirements that arrive are exactly the ones a straight line cannot express. If the first retrieval finds nothing useful, try a different query strategy — that is a retry with state. Classify the request first, and send it down one of three different paths — that is a branch. Generate an answer, check it against the sources, and if the check fails, generate again with the criticism included — that is a loop, and a line has no loops. Pause here, wait for a human to approve the action, and resume tomorrow from where you stopped — that is durable state, and a function call has none. You can express all of these in a chain, with conditional runnables and callbacks and branch objects, and people did. What you end up with is a control-flow graph written sideways in a composition syntax that was designed for pipelines. It works and it is hard to read, hard to test, and hard to reason about, because the actual structure of the computation is scattered across nested runnables instead of being written down anywhere. That experience — a whole community discovering that their applications were graphs and their tool was a pipe — is the direct reason the next slide exists. LangGraph is not a competitor to LangChain; it is the same team saying that the primitive was wrong once applications stopped being linear, and that the honest thing to do is make the graph explicit.",
        "narration_ne": "LangChain को केन्द्रीय विचार composition हो, र यो राम्रो विचार हो। सबै कुरा runnable हो — prompt template, model, output parser, retriever — र runnable pipe operator ले जोडिन्छन्। Prompt pipe model pipe parser। बायाँबाट दायाँ पढ, data बग्छ, र पूरै कुराको एउटै interface छ त्यसैले तिमी कुनै पनि टुक्रा साट्न सक्छौ। रैखिक pipeline का लागि यो साँच्चै रमाइलो छ, र यसले ढाँचा, बोलाउने, streaming र parsing वरिपरिको साँचो मात्राको boilerplate हटाउँछ। यो डिजाइन आफ्नो समयका लागि राम्रो मिल्दो थियो, र कारण ख्याल गर्न लायक छ। लगभग हरेक सुरुको LLM अनुप्रयोग रैखिक थियो। प्रयोगकर्ताको प्रश्न लेऊ। केही कागजात ल्याऊ। prompt मा हाल। model बोलाऊ। output parse गर। फर्काऊ। त्यो पाँच स्टेसन भएको सीधा रेखा हो, र chain सीधा रेखाका लागि ठ्याक्कै सही अमूर्तता हो। यो बनाउनेहरू गलत थिएनन्। अनि अनुप्रयोग महत्त्वाकाङ्क्षी भए, र आउने आवश्यकता ठ्याक्कै ती हुन् जुन सीधा रेखाले व्यक्त गर्न सक्दैन। पहिलो retrieval ले काम लाग्ने केही भेटेन भने, फरक प्रश्न रणनीति प्रयास गर — त्यो state सहितको retry हो। पहिले अनुरोध वर्गीकरण गर, र तीन फरक बाटोमध्ये एउटामा पठाऊ — त्यो हाँगो हो। जवाफ बनाऊ, स्रोतसँग जाँच, र जाँच असफल भए आलोचनासहित फेरि बनाऊ — त्यो loop हो, र रेखामा loop हुँदैन। यहाँ रोक, मान्छेले काम स्वीकृत गर्न कुर, र भोलि रोकिएकै ठाउँबाट सुरु गर — त्यो टिकाउ state हो, र function call सँग त्यो हुँदैन। तिमी यी सबै chain मा, conditional runnable र callback र branch object ले व्यक्त गर्न सक्छौ, र मानिसले गरे। तिमीले अन्ततः पाउने कुरा pipeline का लागि बनाइएको composition वाक्यरचनामा तेर्सो लेखिएको नियन्त्रण-प्रवाहको graph हो। यसले काम गर्छ र यो पढ्न गाह्रो, परीक्षण गर्न गाह्रो, र तर्क गर्न गाह्रो छ, किनभने गणनाको वास्तविक संरचना कतै लेखिनुको सट्टा नेस्ट गरिएका runnable भरि छरिएको छ। त्यो अनुभव — पूरै समुदायले आफ्ना अनुप्रयोग graph रहेछन् र आफ्नो औजार pipe रहेछ भन्ने पत्ता लगाउनु — अर्को slide हुनुको सिधा कारण हो। LangGraph LangChain को प्रतिस्पर्धी होइन; यो उही टोलीले भनेको हो कि अनुप्रयोग रैखिक हुन छाडेपछि primitive गलत थियो, र इमानदार काम graph लाई स्पष्ट बनाउनु हो।"
      },
      {
        "heading": "LangGraph: state, nodes, edges, cycles",
        "bullets": [
          "You declare a <b>state</b> object — a typed dictionary that every step reads and updates. Nodes are functions from state to state; edges say which node runs next; <b>conditional edges</b> route on the state's contents.",
          "<b>Cycles are first-class</b>, which is the whole point. The agent loop from the last lesson is two nodes and an edge back — express it directly instead of hiding a while-loop inside a chain.",
          "<b>Checkpointing</b> makes state durable: persist after every node and a run can be interrupted, inspected, resumed tomorrow, or rewound and replayed from a different point. Human-in-the-loop becomes an edge, not a rewrite.",
          "The trade is verbosity. You write more setup than a chain needs — and you get a structure you can draw, test node by node, and reason about, which is what long-running systems actually need."
        ],
        "narration_en": "LangGraph's model is a state machine, and if that phrase sounds unfashionable it is because state machines are old, well understood and correct for this problem. Three concepts and you have it. First, state: you declare a typed object, usually a dictionary with a schema, that represents everything the run knows. Messages so far, retrieved documents, the current draft, a retry counter, whatever your application needs. Every step reads it and returns updates to it. Second, nodes: a node is a function that takes the state and returns a partial update. One node retrieves. One node calls the model. One node validates. They are ordinary functions and you can unit test them, which chains made awkward. Third, edges: an edge says which node runs after which. And the important variant is the conditional edge — a function that looks at the current state and returns the name of the next node. That is your branching, and notice that it is a plain Python function you can read and test, not a configuration object. Now the feature that justifies the whole redesign: cycles are first class. An edge can point backwards. The agent loop from the last lesson — call the model, run a tool, go back to the model — is two nodes and an edge returning to the first, and you write it exactly like that. Compare hiding a while-loop inside a chain abstraction that was designed for pipelines. The graph makes the loop visible in the structure, which means you can see it, draw it, and put a step counter on it in the obvious place. Then checkpointing, which is the feature teams end up depending on most and rarely anticipate. Because the state is an explicit object, LangGraph can persist it after every node. That makes a run durable, and durability unlocks a set of things that are painful otherwise. You can interrupt a run and resume it hours later. You can inspect the exact state at step four when something went wrong at step seven. You can rewind to an earlier checkpoint, change something, and replay from there. And human-in-the-loop stops being an architectural problem: pausing for approval before a destructive action is a node that interrupts, and resuming is a call with the approval in the state. In a chain, adding that means restructuring; in a graph it is an edge. The trade is verbosity, and it is a genuine trade. A LangGraph agent is more setup code than the equivalent chain — the state schema, the nodes, the edges, the compile step. What you get for it is a structure you can draw on a whiteboard and that matches the code exactly, nodes you can test individually, and a run you can stop and inspect. For a demo that is overhead. For a system that runs for months and that somebody other than you has to debug, it is the difference between maintainable and not.",
        "narration_ne": "LangGraph को model state मेसिन हो, र त्यो वाक्यांश पुरानो लाग्छ भने त्यो किनभने state मेसिन पुराना, राम्ररी बुझिएका र यो समस्याका लागि सही छन्। तीन अवधारणा अनि तिमीसँग यो छ। पहिलो, state: तिमी प्रकारसहितको object घोषणा गर्छौ, प्रायः schema भएको शब्दकोश, जसले run लाई थाहा भएको सबै प्रतिनिधित्व गर्छ। अहिलेसम्मका सन्देश, retrieve भएका कागजात, अहिलेको मस्यौदा, retry गणक, तिम्रो अनुप्रयोगलाई जे चाहिन्छ। हरेक पाइलाले यो पढ्छ र यसमा अद्यावधिक फर्काउँछ। दोस्रो, node: node त्यस्तो function हो जसले state लिन्छ र आंशिक अद्यावधिक फर्काउँछ। एउटा node ले retrieve गर्छ। एउटाले model बोलाउँछ। एउटाले प्रमाणित गर्छ। ती साधारण function हुन् र तिमी unit test गर्न सक्छौ, जुन chain ले अप्ठ्यारो बनाएको थियो। तेस्रो, edge: edge ले कुनपछि कुन node चल्छ भन्छ। र महत्त्वपूर्ण भेद conditional edge हो — त्यस्तो function जसले अहिलेको state हेर्छ र अर्को node को नाम फर्काउँछ। त्यो तिम्रो हाँगो हो, र ख्याल गर त्यो तिमीले पढ्न र परीक्षण गर्न सक्ने सादा Python function हो, configuration object होइन। अब त्यो विशेषता जसले पूरै पुनर्डिजाइन न्यायोचित बनाउँछ: cycle प्रथम श्रेणीका छन्। Edge ले पछाडि देखाउन सक्छ। अघिल्लो पाठको agent loop — model बोलाऊ, tool चलाऊ, model मा फर्क — दुई node र पहिलोमा फर्कने edge हो, र तिमी ठ्याक्कै त्यसै लेख्छौ। pipeline का लागि बनाइएको chain अमूर्तताभित्र while-loop लुकाउनुसँग तुलना गर। Graph ले loop लाई संरचनामा देखिने बनाउँछ, अर्थात् तिमी त्यो देख्न, कोर्न, र स्पष्ट ठाउँमा पाइला गणक राख्न सक्छौ। अनि checkpointing, जुन टोलीले सबैभन्दा बढी भर पर्ने र विरलै अनुमान गर्ने विशेषता हो। State स्पष्ट object भएकाले, LangGraph ले हरेक node पछि यो टिकाउन सक्छ। त्यसले run लाई टिकाउ बनाउँछ, र टिकाउपनले नत्र पीडादायी हुने कुराको समूह खोल्छ। तिमी run रोकेर घण्टौंपछि सुरु गर्न सक्छौ। सात नम्बर पाइलामा केही बिग्रँदा चार नम्बर पाइलाको ठ्याक्कै state जाँच्न सक्छौ। अघिल्लो checkpoint मा फर्केर, केही फेरेर, त्यहाँबाट फेरि बजाउन सक्छौ। र human-in-the-loop architecture को समस्या हुन छाड्छ: विनाशकारी काम अघि स्वीकृतिका लागि रोकिनु अवरोध गर्ने node हो, र सुरु गर्नु state मा स्वीकृतिसहितको call हो। Chain मा, त्यो थप्नुको अर्थ पुनर्संरचना हो; graph मा त्यो edge हो। सट्टा शब्दबाहुल्य हो, र त्यो साँचो सट्टा हो। LangGraph agent बराबरको chain भन्दा बढी setup code हो — state schema, node, edge, compile पाइला। त्यसको बदलामा तिमी whiteboard मा कोर्न सकिने र code सँग ठ्याक्कै मिल्ने संरचना, छुट्टाछुट्टै परीक्षण गर्न सकिने node, र रोकेर जाँच्न सकिने run पाउँछौ। Demo का लागि त्यो अतिरिक्त भार हो। महिनौं चल्ने र तिमीबाहेक अरू कसैले debug गर्नुपर्ने प्रणालीका लागि, त्यो मर्मतयोग्य र नहुनेबीचको फरक हो।"
      },
      {
        "heading": "One needle, or a chart you drew",
        "bullets": [
          "A Log Pose is a beautiful abstraction: it locks onto the next island's magnetism and points. You do not plot anything, you follow the needle, and in the first half of the Grand Line it is enough.",
          "It is also a straight line. It has no opinion about where you want to go, it cannot take you back, and when the needle is wrong you have no chart to check it against. <b>That is a chain.</b>",
          "The New World gives you three needles and no rule for choosing. Now you need a chart you drew: <b>where you are, what you have tried, which route you rejected and why</b> — state, nodes, edges.",
          "And a chart lets you stop, anchor, resume, and go back to a point you passed. <b>That is checkpointing</b> — and it is why the crew that survives the New World is the one that writes things down."
        ],
        "narration_en": "The Log Pose is one of the best abstractions in the whole story, and it is a chain. Think about what it does. It locks onto the magnetic field of the next island and points at it. You do not calculate anything, you do not plot a course, you do not need to know the geography. You follow the needle and you arrive. For the first half of the Grand Line that is genuinely sufficient, and it is why most crews get that far — the abstraction is doing real work and hiding real complexity, which is what a good abstraction does. Now notice its shape, because the shape is the argument. A Log Pose is a straight line with one next step on it. It has no opinion about where you actually want to go — it points where it points, and the route is chosen for you. It cannot take you back to an island you have left. And when the needle is wrong, or the island it locked onto is not the one you needed, you have nothing to check it against, because you never had a chart. You have been navigating by following, and following gives you no way to reason. That is exactly a chain: a good abstraction for a linear journey, and no help at all the moment the journey stops being linear. Then the New World, where the Log Pose grows three needles and gives you no rule for choosing between them. This is the moment the abstraction runs out, and notice that it does not fail loudly — it simply stops answering the question. Three needles is not a route. What you need now is a chart you drew yourself: where you are, which of the three you took, what you found there, which route you rejected and why, and what you will do if this one turns out badly. That is state, nodes and edges, and the reason you need them written down is that the situation is no longer a line — it is a set of choices with consequences that you may have to revisit. And a chart gives you one more thing the needle never could, which is the ability to stop. You can drop anchor, mark your position, and take up the voyage next week from exactly where you left it. You can go back to a fork you passed and take the other branch. You can hand the chart to somebody else and they can see what you were doing and why. That is checkpointing, and it is the difference between a voyage that can survive an interruption and one that has to be started again from the beginning. It is not an accident that in the New World the crews that do well are the ones that write things down. The needle is faster while the route is simple. The chart is the only thing that works once it is not.",
        "narration_ne": "Log Pose पूरै कथाका उत्कृष्ट अमूर्ततामध्ये एक हो, र त्यो chain हो। यसले के गर्छ सोच। यसले अर्को टापुको चुम्बकीय क्षेत्रमा अड्किन्छ र त्यतै देखाउँछ। तिमी केही गणना गर्दैनौ, बाटो कोर्दैनौ, भूगोल जान्नु पर्दैन। तिमी सुई पछ्याउँछौ र पुग्छौ। Grand Line को पहिलो आधाका लागि त्यो साँच्चै पर्याप्त छ, र त्यसैले अधिकांश crew त्यति टाढा पुग्छन् — अमूर्तताले साँचो काम गर्दैछ र साँचो जटिलता लुकाउँदैछ, जुन राम्रो अमूर्तताले गर्ने काम हो। अब यसको आकार ख्याल गर, किनभने आकार नै तर्क हो। Log Pose एउटै अर्को पाइला भएको सीधा रेखा हो। तिमी साँच्चै कहाँ जान चाहन्छौ भन्नेमा यसको कुनै राय छैन — यसले जता देखाउँछ त्यतै देखाउँछ, र बाटो तिम्रा लागि छानिन्छ। यसले तिमीलाई छोडेको टापुमा फर्काउन सक्दैन। र सुई गलत हुँदा, वा यो अड्किएको टापु तिमीलाई चाहिने थिएन भने, तिमीसँग जाँच्ने केही छैन, किनभने तिमीसँग कहिल्यै chart थिएन। तिमी पछ्याएर बाटो लगाउँदै थियौ, र पछ्याउनुले तिमीलाई तर्क गर्ने कुनै उपाय दिँदैन। त्यो ठ्याक्कै chain हो: रैखिक यात्राका लागि राम्रो अमूर्तता, र यात्रा रैखिक हुन छाडेको क्षणमा कुनै मद्दत छैन। अनि New World, जहाँ Log Pose मा तीन सुई उम्रन्छन् र तीबीच छान्ने कुनै नियम दिँदैन। यो त्यो क्षण हो जब अमूर्तता सकिन्छ, र ख्याल गर यो ठूलो स्वरमा असफल हुँदैन — यसले प्रश्नको जवाफ दिन छाड्छ, बस्। तीन सुई बाटो होइन। अब तिमीलाई चाहिने कुरा तिमी आफैंले कोरेको chart हो: तिमी कहाँ छौ, तीनमध्ये कुन लियौ, त्यहाँ के भेट्यौ, कुन बाटो अस्वीकार गर्‍यौ र किन, र यो नराम्रो निस्क्यो भने के गर्नेछौ। त्यो state, node र edge हो, र ती लेख्नुपर्ने कारण अवस्था अब रेखा होइन — त्यो परिणामसहितका छनोटको समूह हो जुन तिमीले फेरि हेर्नुपर्न सक्छ। र chart ले तिमीलाई सुईले कहिल्यै दिन नसक्ने अझ एउटा कुरा दिन्छ, जुन हो रोक्न सक्ने क्षमता। तिमी लङ्गर हाल्न सक्छौ, आफ्नो स्थान चिन्ह लगाउन सक्छौ, र अर्को हप्ता ठ्याक्कै छोडेकै ठाउँबाट यात्रा उठाउन सक्छौ। तिमी नाघेको दोबाटोमा फर्केर अर्को हाँगो लिन सक्छौ। तिमी chart अर्कैलाई दिन सक्छौ र तिनले तिमी के गर्दै थियौ र किन देख्न सक्छन्। त्यो checkpointing हो, र त्यो अवरोध सहन सक्ने यात्रा र सुरुबाटै फेरि सुरु गर्नुपर्ने यात्राबीचको फरक हो। New World मा राम्रो गर्ने crew ती हुन् जसले कुरा लेख्छन् — त्यो संयोग होइन। बाटो सरल हुँदासम्म सुई छिटो छ। नरहेपछि काम गर्ने एउटै कुरा chart हो।"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Choosing: adopt, or write sixty lines",
        "bullets": [
          "<b>Write it yourself</b> when the flow is simple and you want to understand it: one RAG pipeline, a small agent loop, a prototype. Sixty lines with no dependency is often the right engineering answer, not a purist one.",
          "<b>Reach for a framework</b> when you need the boring surface area — many loaders, many providers, many stores — or when the graph is genuinely complex, or when you need durable state and human-in-the-loop.",
          "<b>Adopt tracing early regardless.</b> Whatever you build, you need to see every prompt, tool call and cost for one request in one timeline. This is the piece nobody regrets and everybody adds too late.",
          "<b>Keep the model boundary yours either way.</b> Frameworks change fast; if your provider calls already sit behind your own thin interface, adopting or dropping one stays a contained decision."
        ],
        "narration_en": "Let us finish with a decision procedure, because this is a question every team argues about and it has a reasonably clean answer. Write it yourself when the flow is simple and you want to understand it. A single RAG pipeline is about forty lines. A small agent loop is about sixty. If that is what your application is, writing it directly gives you no dependency, no version churn, complete visibility into every prompt, and a codebase that a new engineer can read in one sitting. This is not purism — it is often the correct engineering answer, and the fact that a framework exists does not create an obligation to use it. Reach for a framework in three situations. First, when you need the boring surface area: you have to ingest twelve document formats, or support four model providers, or you are evaluating three vector stores. That is exactly the work a framework has already done, and reimplementing it is a poor use of a month. Second, when your control flow is genuinely a complex graph — several branches, retries with different strategies, sub-agents, and a real state object that many steps read and update. At that point you are going to build a state machine whether you like it or not, and using one that already has checkpointing and tracing is better than growing your own accidentally. Third, and this one is decisive on its own: when you need durability. If a run must survive a process restart, or pause for a human and resume tomorrow, or be rewound and replayed, then persisted state is the requirement and you should use something that has it rather than building a checkpointing layer as a side project. Third piece of advice, independent of the framework question: adopt tracing early. Whatever you build, you need to be able to look at one request and see every prompt that was sent, every tool that was called, every intermediate result and the cost of each, on one timeline. Debugging a multi-step LLM system without that is guessing, and every team I have seen adds it late and wishes they had added it first. It is also the part of these frameworks that stands on its own — you can use a tracing tool with hand-written code perfectly well. And the last one, which ties back to Part Six: keep the model boundary yours either way. Whether you adopt a framework or not, your provider calls should sit behind your own thin interface. Frameworks in this space move fast, some of them will not exist in three years, and a codebase where the framework's abstractions have reached every file is a codebase that cannot leave. If the framework sits on your side of a boundary you control, then adopting it is a reversible decision — and reversible decisions are the ones you are allowed to make quickly.",
        "narration_ne": "निर्णय प्रक्रियाले टुङ्ग्याऔं, किनभने हरेक टोलीले यसमा बहस गर्छ र यसको उचित सफा जवाफ छ। प्रवाह सरल छ र तिमी बुझ्न चाहन्छौ भने आफैं लेख। एउटा RAG pipeline लगभग चालीस हरफ हो। सानो agent loop लगभग साठी। तिम्रो अनुप्रयोग त्यही हो भने, सिधै लेख्दा तिमीलाई कुनै निर्भरता, कुनै संस्करणको हल्लाखल्ला हुँदैन, हरेक prompt मा पूरा दृश्यता हुन्छ, र नयाँ इन्जिनियरले एकै बसाइमा पढ्न सक्ने codebase हुन्छ। यो शुद्धतावाद होइन — यो प्राय: सही इन्जिनियरिङ जवाफ हो, र framework अस्तित्वमा छ भन्दैमा प्रयोग गर्नुपर्ने बाध्यता बन्दैन। तीन अवस्थामा framework तिर हात बढाऊ। पहिलो, तिमीलाई नीरस सतह क्षेत्र चाहिँदा: तिमीले बाह्र कागजात ढाँचा भित्र्याउनुपर्छ, वा चार model प्रदायक समर्थन गर्नुपर्छ, वा तीन vector भण्डार मूल्याङ्कन गर्दैछौ। त्यो ठ्याक्कै framework ले पहिल्यै गरिसकेको काम हो, र फेरि लेख्नु एक महिनाको खराब प्रयोग हो। दोस्रो, तिम्रो नियन्त्रण प्रवाह साँच्चै जटिल graph हुँदा — कैयौं हाँगा, फरक रणनीतिसहितका retry, उप-agent, र धेरै पाइलाले पढ्ने र अद्यावधिक गर्ने साँचो state object। त्यो बिन्दुमा तिमी मन परे नपरे state मेसिन बनाउँदै छौ, र पहिल्यै checkpointing र tracing भएको प्रयोग गर्नु आफ्नै संयोगले उमार्नुभन्दा राम्रो हो। तेस्रो, र यो आफैंमा निर्णायक छ: तिमीलाई टिकाउपन चाहिँदा। Run process पुनःसुरु सहनुपर्छ, वा मान्छेका लागि रोकिएर भोलि सुरु हुनुपर्छ, वा फर्केर फेरि बज्नुपर्छ भने, टिकाउ state आवश्यकता हो र तिमीले checkpointing को तह छेउको परियोजनाका रूपमा बनाउनुको सट्टा त्यो भएको कुरा प्रयोग गर्नुपर्छ। तेस्रो सल्लाह, framework को प्रश्नबाट स्वतन्त्र: tracing चाँडै अपनाऊ। तिमीले जे बनाए पनि, तिमीले एउटा अनुरोध हेरेर पठाइएको हरेक prompt, बोलाइएको हरेक tool, हरेक बीचको नतिजा र हरेकको लागत एउटै timeline मा देख्न सक्नुपर्छ। त्यसबिना बहु-पाइलेको LLM प्रणाली debug गर्नु अनुमान लगाउनु हो, र मैले देखेका हरेक टोलीले यो ढिलो थप्छन् र पहिले थपेको भए हुन्थ्यो भन्छन्। यो यी framework को आफैंमा उभिने भाग पनि हो — तिमी हातले लेखेको code सँग tracing उपकरण राम्ररी प्रयोग गर्न सक्छौ। र अन्तिम, जुन भाग छसँग जोडिन्छ: जे भए पनि model को सिमाना तिम्रै राख। तिमीले framework अपनाए पनि नअपनाए पनि, तिम्रा प्रदायक call तिम्रै पातलो interface पछाडि बस्नुपर्छ। यस क्षेत्रका framework छिटो सर्छन्, तीमध्ये केही तीन वर्षमा अस्तित्वमा हुनेछैनन्, र framework का अमूर्तता हरेक file मा पुगेको codebase छोड्न नसकिने codebase हो। Framework तिमीले नियन्त्रण गर्ने सिमानाको तिम्रो पट्टि बस्छ भने, यसलाई अपनाउनु उल्टाउन सकिने निर्णय हो — र उल्टाउन सकिने निर्णय नै तिमीले छिटो गर्न पाइने निर्णय हुन्।"
      }
    ],
  },

