  {
    "id": "multi-framework-agents",
    "title": "Multi-Agent Systems and the Framework Landscape",
    "subtitle_en": "Enies Lobby: nine people, one objective, and almost no communication",
    "subtitle_ne": "Enies Lobby: नौ जना, एउटै उद्देश्य, र लगभग कुनै सञ्चार छैन",
    "intro_en": "what the agent frameworks actually differ on, the three multi-agent patterns worth knowing, the costs the demos leave out, and an honest default",
    "intro_ne": "agent framework साँच्चै केमा फरक छन्, जान्न लायक तीन multi-agent ढाँचा, demo ले छोड्ने लागत, र इमानदार पूर्वनिर्धारित",
    "slides": [
      {
        "heading": "The landscape, and what actually separates them",
        "bullets": [
          "<b>LangGraph</b> — an explicit state graph. Most control, most setup, durable checkpointing. Right when the flow is complex and long-running.",
          "<b>CrewAI</b> — agents as roles with goals, coordinated by a manager. Very fast to a working demo, and the abstraction is a team, which fits some problems and forces others.",
          "<b>AutoGen</b> — agents as participants in a conversation, including a code-executing one. Strong for research and for tasks that are genuinely dialogue-shaped.",
          "<b>OpenAI Agents SDK / Google ADK</b> — thin, provider-aligned, few concepts. <b>They differ mostly in what they force you to make explicit</b>, not in what they can express."
        ],
        "narration_en": "There are a lot of agent frameworks and the marketing makes them sound more different than they are. Underneath, every one of them is the loop you wrote two lessons ago: a model, a set of tools, a dispatch step, an observation, and a termination condition. None of them can express something the others cannot. What they genuinely differ on is which decisions they force you to make explicitly and which they make for you, and if you evaluate them on that axis the choice becomes straightforward. LangGraph you already know: an explicit state graph, nodes and edges you declare, durable checkpointing. It gives you the most control and asks for the most setup, and it is the right answer when the control flow is complex, when runs are long, or when you need to pause and resume. Its abstraction is a state machine, which is a very general shape, and generality is why it is verbose. CrewAI takes a different bet: the abstraction is a team. You define agents as roles — a researcher, a writer, an editor — each with a goal and a backstory, and a manager coordinates them. It is remarkably fast to a working demo, and the reason is that the abstraction does a lot for you. That is also the risk: if your problem genuinely looks like a team of specialists producing a document, it fits beautifully; if it does not, you will find yourself describing your problem as a team of specialists in order to use the tool, which is the abstraction leading rather than serving. AutoGen models agents as participants in a conversation, and it includes an agent that executes code, which makes it strong for research and analysis workflows where the task really is a dialogue that produces and runs programs. Microsoft's rewrite is more production-oriented than the original research release, so check which generation you are reading about. And then the thin provider-aligned ones — the OpenAI Agents SDK, Google's ADK. Few concepts, close to the underlying API, less magic, correspondingly less help when your flow gets complicated. If you are already committed to one provider and your agent is simple, these are often the least-overhead choice. So the honest summary of the landscape: they all wrap the same loop. Choose by how much structure you want imposed and how much you want to write yourself, and by whether their central metaphor — a graph, a team, a conversation — matches the shape of your actual problem. If none of them does, that is a signal to write the loop yourself, not to pick the closest one and bend your problem to fit it.",
        "narration_ne": "धेरै agent framework छन् र प्रचारले तिनलाई भएभन्दा बढी फरक सुनाउँछ। मुनि, तीमध्ये हरेक तिमीले दुई पाठअघि लेखेको loop हो: एउटा model, tool को समूह, dispatch पाइला, अवलोकन, र अन्त्यको सर्त। कुनैले पनि अरूले व्यक्त गर्न नसक्ने कुरा व्यक्त गर्न सक्दैन। तिनी साँच्चै केमा फरक छन् भने कुन निर्णय तिमीलाई स्पष्ट गर्न बाध्य पार्छन् र कुन तिम्रा लागि आफैं गर्छन्, र तिमीले त्यही अक्षमा मूल्याङ्कन गर्‍यौ भने छनोट सिधा हुन्छ। LangGraph तिमीलाई पहिल्यै थाहा छ: स्पष्ट state graph, तिमीले घोषणा गर्ने node र edge, टिकाउ checkpointing। यसले तिमीलाई सबैभन्दा बढी नियन्त्रण दिन्छ र सबैभन्दा बढी setup माग्छ, र नियन्त्रण प्रवाह जटिल हुँदा, run लामो हुँदा, वा तिमीलाई रोकेर सुरु गर्नुपर्दा यो सही जवाफ हो। यसको अमूर्तता state मेसिन हो, जुन धेरै सामान्य आकार हो, र सामान्यता नै यो शब्दबहुल हुनुको कारण हो। CrewAI फरक दाउ लगाउँछ: अमूर्तता एउटा टोली हो। तिमी agent लाई भूमिकाका रूपमा परिभाषित गर्छौ — अनुसन्धानकर्ता, लेखक, सम्पादक — हरेकको लक्ष्य र पृष्ठभूमिसहित, र manager ले तिनलाई मिलाउँछ। चल्ने demo सम्म पुग्न यो उल्लेखनीय रूपमा छिटो छ, र कारण अमूर्तताले तिम्रा लागि धेरै काम गर्छ। त्यही जोखिम पनि हो: तिम्रो समस्या साँच्चै कागजात बनाउने विशेषज्ञको टोलीजस्तो देखिन्छ भने, यो राम्ररी मिल्छ; देखिँदैन भने, तिमी औजार प्रयोग गर्न आफ्नो समस्यालाई विशेषज्ञको टोलीका रूपमा वर्णन गर्दै हुनेछौ, जुन अमूर्तताले सेवा गर्नुको सट्टा डोर्‍याएको हो। AutoGen ले agent लाई कुराकानीका सहभागीका रूपमा model गर्छ, र यसमा code चलाउने agent छ, जसले यसलाई अनुसन्धान र विश्लेषणका प्रवाहका लागि बलियो बनाउँछ जहाँ कार्य साँच्चै program बनाउने र चलाउने संवाद हो। Microsoft को पुनर्लेखन मूल अनुसन्धान संस्करणभन्दा बढी उत्पादनमुखी छ, त्यसैले तिमी कुन पुस्ताबारे पढ्दैछौ जाँच। र अनि पातला प्रदायकसँग मिल्ने — OpenAI Agents SDK, Google को ADK। थोरै अवधारणा, अन्तर्निहित API नजिक, कम जादू, तदनुसार तिम्रो प्रवाह जटिल हुँदा कम मद्दत। तिमी पहिल्यै एउटा प्रदायकमा प्रतिबद्ध छौ र तिम्रो agent सरल छ भने, यी प्राय: सबैभन्दा कम भारको छनोट हुन्। त्यसैले परिदृश्यको इमानदार सार: सबैले उही loop बेर्छन्। कति संरचना लादिएको चाहन्छौ र कति आफैं लेख्न चाहन्छौ, र तिनको केन्द्रीय रूपक — graph, टोली, कुराकानी — तिम्रो साँचो समस्याको आकारसँग मिल्छ कि मिल्दैन, त्यसैले छान। कुनैले मिल्दैन भने, त्यो loop आफैं लेख्ने सङ्केत हो, नजिकको छानेर आफ्नो समस्या त्यसमा बटार्ने होइन।"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Three multi-agent patterns worth knowing",
        "bullets": [
          "<b>Supervisor</b>: one router receives the task and delegates to specialists, each with its own prompt and its own small tool set. The most useful pattern, because it is really just routing plus scoping.",
          "<b>Sequential pipeline</b>: agent A's output is agent B's input — research, then draft, then edit. Predictable and testable, and honestly it is a workflow with model calls in it, which is a compliment.",
          "<b>Debate / critique</b>: one agent produces, another attacks, a third decides. Genuinely improves quality on open-ended work, and costs three or more times as much for it.",
          "<b>The real benefit is scoping, not intelligence.</b> Five focused agents beat one agent with twenty tools because each has a shorter prompt and fewer wrong choices available — not because they collaborate."
        ],
        "narration_en": "Multi-agent means several model instances with different prompts and different tools, cooperating on one task. Three patterns cover almost everything people actually build, and it is worth being precise about why they help, because the usual explanation is wrong. The supervisor pattern is the most useful one. A router agent receives the task, works out which specialist should handle it, and delegates — with each specialist having its own focused prompt and its own small set of tools. A support system might route to a billing agent, a technical agent and an account agent. This is the pattern that most often earns its complexity, and the reason is not that the agents collaborate cleverly. It is scoping. Each specialist has a short prompt about one domain and four tools instead of a long prompt about six domains and twenty tools, and a shorter prompt with fewer available wrong choices is measurably more reliable. Sequential pipelines are the second pattern: agent A researches, agent B drafts from the research, agent C edits the draft. Each step is specialised and the handoffs are explicit. This is predictable, testable, and easy to debug because you can inspect the artifact between any two stages. It is also, honestly, a workflow with model calls in it — and I mean that as praise rather than criticism, because that is exactly what you should prefer when the sequence is knowable. Debate and critique is the third. One agent produces something, a second attacks it, and either the first revises or a third adjudicates. This genuinely improves quality on open-ended work — arguments get stronger when something looks for holes in them, and the effect is real and measurable on writing and analysis. It also costs three or more times as much and takes three times as long, so it belongs on work where quality matters more than latency and where a human would otherwise be doing the reviewing. Now the sentence to take away, because it corrects the most common misconception in this area. The benefit of multi-agent systems is scoping, not emergent intelligence. Five agents do not think better together than one agent thinks alone — they are the same model, and nothing about running it five times creates a capability that was not there. What multi-agent buys you is that each instance has a narrower job, a shorter prompt, a smaller tool set, and therefore fewer ways to go wrong. Every real benefit you will measure traces back to that. Which is a useful thing to know, because it tells you when the pattern will not help: if your task cannot be cleanly divided into narrower jobs, adding agents adds coordination cost and buys you nothing.",
        "narration_ne": "Multi-agent को अर्थ फरक prompt र फरक tool भएका कैयौं model instance एउटै कार्यमा सहकार्य गर्नु हो। तीन ढाँचाले मानिसले साँच्चै बनाउने लगभग सबै ढाक्छ, र तिनले किन मद्दत गर्छन् भन्नेमा ठीक हुनु सार्थक छ, किनभने सामान्य व्याख्या गलत छ। Supervisor ढाँचा सबैभन्दा उपयोगी हो। Router agent ले कार्य पाउँछ, कुन विशेषज्ञले सम्हाल्ने पत्ता लगाउँछ, र सुम्पन्छ — हरेक विशेषज्ञसँग आफ्नै केन्द्रित prompt र आफ्नै सानो tool समूह हुन्छ। Support प्रणालीले billing agent, प्राविधिक agent र खाता agent मा route गर्न सक्छ। यही ढाँचाले प्राय: आफ्नो जटिलता कमाउँछ, र कारण agent ले चतुरतापूर्वक सहकार्य गर्छन् भन्ने होइन। त्यो scoping हो। हरेक विशेषज्ञसँग छ क्षेत्रबारे लामो prompt र बीस tool को सट्टा एउटा क्षेत्रबारे छोटो prompt र चार tool हुन्छन्, र कम गलत छनोट उपलब्ध भएको छोटो prompt नाप्न सकिने गरी बढी भरपर्दो हुन्छ। Sequential pipeline दोस्रो ढाँचा हो: agent A ले अनुसन्धान गर्छ, agent B ले अनुसन्धानबाट मस्यौदा बनाउँछ, agent C ले मस्यौदा सम्पादन गर्छ। हरेक पाइला विशेषीकृत छ र हस्तान्तरण स्पष्ट छन्। यो अनुमानयोग्य, परीक्षणयोग्य, र debug गर्न सजिलो छ किनभने तिमी कुनै पनि दुई चरणबीचको वस्तु जाँच्न सक्छौ। यो, इमानदारीपूर्वक, model call भएको workflow पनि हो — र म यो आलोचना होइन प्रशंसाका रूपमा भन्छु, किनभने क्रम जान्न सकिँदा तिमीले रुचाउनुपर्ने ठ्याक्कै त्यही हो। बहस र आलोचना तेस्रो हो। एउटा agent ले केही बनाउँछ, दोस्रोले त्यसमाथि प्रहार गर्छ, र या पहिलोले संशोधन गर्छ या तेस्रोले फैसला गर्छ। यसले खुला कार्यमा साँच्चै गुणस्तर सुधार्छ — कसैले प्वाल खोज्दा तर्क बलिया हुन्छन्, र असर लेखाइ र विश्लेषणमा साँचो र नाप्न सकिने छ। यसले तीन वा बढी गुणा खर्च पनि गर्छ र तीन गुणा समय लिन्छ, त्यसैले यो त्यस्तो कामको हो जहाँ latency भन्दा गुणस्तर महत्त्वपूर्ण छ र जहाँ नत्र मान्छेले समीक्षा गरिरहेको हुन्थ्यो। अब लैजाने वाक्य, किनभने यसले यस क्षेत्रको सबैभन्दा सामान्य भ्रम सच्याउँछ। Multi-agent प्रणालीको फाइदा scoping हो, उदीयमान बुद्धि होइन। पाँच agent ले सँगै एउटा agent ले एक्लै सोच्नेभन्दा राम्रो सोच्दैनन् — ती उही model हुन्, र त्यसलाई पाँच पटक चलाउँदा नभएको क्षमता बन्दैन। Multi-agent ले तिमीलाई किन्ने कुरा हरेक instance को साँघुरो काम, छोटो prompt, सानो tool समूह, र त्यसैले गलत हुने कम बाटो हो। तिमीले नाप्ने हरेक साँचो फाइदा त्यहीँ पुग्छ। जुन जान्न उपयोगी छ, किनभने यसले ढाँचाले कहिले मद्दत गर्दैन भन्छ: तिम्रो कार्य सफासँग साँघुरा कामहरूमा बाँड्न सकिँदैन भने, agent थप्दा समन्वयको लागत थपिन्छ र तिमीले केही पाउँदैनौ।"
      },
      {
        "heading": "The costs the demo leaves out",
        "bullets": [
          "<b>Cost multiplies.</b> Each agent carries its own prompt and its own history, so a five-agent system on one task is often five to ten times the tokens of a single well-scoped agent.",
          "<b>Reliability multiplies downward.</b> Chained agents at 90% each give 59% over five steps. Adding agents adds steps, and steps multiply — the same arithmetic that limits long agent loops limits wide ones.",
          "<b>Debugging gets qualitatively harder.</b> With one agent you read a trace. With five you have to work out which one went wrong, on what it was handed, and whether the handoff itself was the bug.",
          "<b>Handoffs lose information silently.</b> Agent A knew something it did not think worth summarising, agent B needed it, and nothing anywhere reports that the loss occurred."
        ],
        "narration_en": "Multi-agent systems demo extremely well, and the demo omits four costs that decide whether one is a good idea. Cost, first, and it multiplies faster than people expect. Each agent has its own system prompt, its own tool schemas, and its own conversation history, and every one of those is tokens on every call that agent makes. A five-agent system working on one task frequently uses five to ten times the tokens of a single well-scoped agent doing the same job, and the supervisor pattern adds another layer because the router is itself a model call. If your justification for the architecture is quality, measure whether you got quality proportional to a tenfold bill. Reliability, second, and it is the same arithmetic that limited long agent loops, now applied sideways. If each agent in a chain is ninety percent reliable, five of them in sequence give you fifty-nine percent. Adding agents adds steps, and steps multiply. So a multi-agent system is not more robust than a single agent by virtue of having more parts — it is usually less robust, and each additional specialist has to earn its place by removing more error than it introduces. Debugging, third, and this is the one that hurts in practice. With a single agent, something goes wrong and you read the trace: here is what it thought, here is what it called, here is what came back. With five agents you first have to work out which agent produced the bad output, then what it was actually handed by the agent before it, then whether the fault was in that agent's reasoning or in the handoff. The trace is now several interleaved traces, and reproducing the failure requires reproducing the whole choreography. This is why good tracing is not optional for multi-agent work — it is the only thing standing between you and guessing. Fourth, and it is the subtlest: information loss at the handoffs. Agent A finished its research and wrote a summary for agent B. Somewhere in the material A read was a caveat it did not think worth including. B never sees it, produces a confident answer that the caveat would have changed, and nothing in the system reports that anything was lost — because from B's perspective nothing was. Every handoff is a lossy compression step performed by a model that does not know what the next agent will need. Adding agents adds handoffs, and each one is a place where something quietly falls out of the system. So the discipline is: start with one agent, and add a second only when you can name the specific failure it fixes and measure that the fix worked. Growing a multi-agent system by intuition produces something expensive, fragile and unexplainable, and it usually performs worse than the single agent it replaced.",
        "narration_ne": "Multi-agent प्रणालीले अत्यन्त राम्रो demo दिन्छन्, र demo ले चार लागत छोड्छ जसले यो राम्रो विचार हो कि होइन तय गर्छन्। पहिले लागत, र यो मानिसले सोचेभन्दा छिटो गुणन हुन्छ। हरेक agent सँग आफ्नै system prompt, आफ्नै tool schema, र आफ्नै कुराकानीको इतिहास हुन्छ, र ती हरेक त्यो agent ले गर्ने हरेक call मा token हुन्। एउटै कार्यमा काम गर्ने पाँच-agent प्रणालीले प्राय: उही काम गर्ने राम्ररी scope गरिएको एउटा agent भन्दा पाँचदेखि दस गुणा token प्रयोग गर्छ, र supervisor ढाँचाले अर्को तह थप्छ किनभने router आफैं model call हो। तिम्रो architecture को औचित्य गुणस्तर हो भने, दस गुणा बिलको समानुपातिक गुणस्तर पायौ कि नाप। दोस्रो भरपर्दोपन, र यो लामो agent loop लाई सीमित गर्ने उही अङ्कगणित हो, अब तेर्सो लागू। शृङ्खलाको हरेक agent नब्बे प्रतिशत भरपर्दो छ भने, क्रमैसँग पाँचले तिमीलाई उनान्साठी प्रतिशत दिन्छन्। Agent थप्दा पाइला थपिन्छन्, र पाइला गुणन हुन्छन्। त्यसैले multi-agent प्रणाली बढी भाग भएकैले एउटा agent भन्दा बढी बलियो हुँदैन — यो प्रायः कम बलियो हुन्छ, र हरेक थप विशेषज्ञले आफूले ल्याउनेभन्दा बढी त्रुटि हटाएर आफ्नो ठाउँ कमाउनुपर्छ। तेस्रो debugging, र व्यवहारमा यही दुखाउँछ। एउटा agent सँग, केही बिग्रन्छ र तिमी trace पढ्छौ: यसले के सोच्यो, के बोलायो, के फर्कियो। पाँच agent सँग तिमीले पहिले कुन agent ले नराम्रो output बनायो पत्ता लगाउनुपर्छ, अनि अघिल्लो agent ले त्यसलाई साँच्चै के दियो, अनि दोष त्यो agent को तर्कमा थियो कि हस्तान्तरणमा। Trace अब कैयौं मिसिएका trace हुन्, र असफलता पुनरुत्पादन गर्न पूरै नृत्यसंरचना पुनरुत्पादन गर्नुपर्छ। त्यसैले multi-agent कामका लागि राम्रो tracing वैकल्पिक होइन — तिमी र अनुमानबीच उभिने एउटै कुरा त्यही हो। चौथो, र सबैभन्दा सूक्ष्म: हस्तान्तरणमा जानकारीको हानि। Agent A ले आफ्नो अनुसन्धान सक्यो र agent B का लागि सारांश लेख्यो। A ले पढेको सामग्रीमा कतै त्यस्तो चेतावनी थियो जुन समावेश गर्न लायक ठानेन। B ले त्यो कहिल्यै देख्दैन, त्यो चेतावनीले फेर्ने आत्मविश्वासी जवाफ बनाउँछ, र प्रणालीको कुनै कुराले केही हरायो भन्ने रिपोर्ट गर्दैन — किनभने B को दृष्टिकोणबाट केही हराएकै थिएन। हरेक हस्तान्तरण अर्को agent लाई के चाहिनेछ थाहा नभएको model ले गरेको हानिकारक सङ्कुचन पाइला हो। Agent थप्दा हस्तान्तरण थपिन्छन्, र हरेक त्यस्तो ठाउँ हो जहाँ केही चुपचाप प्रणालीबाट खस्छ। त्यसैले अनुशासन यो हो: एउटा agent बाट सुरु गर, र दोस्रो तब मात्र थप जब तिमी यसले ठीक पार्ने खास असफलताको नाम भन्न सक्छौ र त्यो उपचारले काम गर्‍यो नाप्न सक्छौ। अन्तर्ज्ञानले multi-agent प्रणाली उमार्दा महँगो, कमजोर र व्याख्या गर्न नसकिने कुरा बन्छ, र त्यसले प्रायः आफूले प्रतिस्थापन गरेको एउटै agent भन्दा नराम्रो प्रदर्शन गर्छ।"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Enies Lobby: nine people, one objective",
        "bullets": [
          "The crew splits at the gate and never regroups until the end. Each takes the fight suited to them — <b>specialisation with almost no coordination</b>, which is what a multi-agent system actually is.",
          "It works because the division is <i>clean</i>. Nobody has to decide who takes the swordsman; the assignment is obvious from who they are. <b>That is a well-scoped tool set and an unambiguous router.</b>",
          "It nearly fails on the handoffs. Nobody knows what anyone else has found, decisions are made on stale information, and the one thing that keeps it together is a single shared signal everybody can see — the burning flag.",
          "<b>The lesson is not that splitting up is powerful.</b> It is that splitting up works when the split is obvious and there is one piece of shared state everyone can read. Otherwise it is nine people improvising."
        ],
        "narration_en": "Enies Lobby is a multi-agent system, and it is a useful one to think with because it shows both halves — why the pattern works and how it nearly comes apart. Watch the structure. The crew arrives at the gate and immediately splits, and they do not regroup until the objective is achieved. Zoro takes the swordsman. Sanji takes the one he can reach fastest. Chopper handles what needs a doctor's knowledge, Nami takes the one who fights with weather, Usopp takes the one nobody expected him to take. Nine people, one objective, and after the first minute almost no communication between them. That is exactly what a multi-agent system is, and stating it that plainly already tells you where the risks are. Now, why does it work? Not because they coordinate brilliantly, because they do not coordinate at all. It works because the division of labour is clean. Nobody has to hold a meeting about who takes the swordsman — the assignment falls out of who each of them is. Each crew member has a narrow, well-understood capability and takes the sub-problem that matches it, and there is no ambiguity to resolve. That is the supervisor pattern with a perfect router: a specialist per task, scoped tightly, chosen without deliberation. When your problem decomposes that cleanly, multi-agent is genuinely the right architecture, and the reason it helps is the reason from the last slide — each of them is doing one narrow thing they are suited for, not because the group is smarter than its members. Now watch where it nearly falls apart, because that is the more useful half. Almost nobody knows what anyone else has found. Robin learns something crucial and there is no way to tell the others. Decisions get made on information that was true twenty minutes ago. People fight battles that are already unnecessary because the situation changed elsewhere. Every one of those is a handoff failure, and they are not caused by anyone being wrong — they are caused by there being no channel. And the thing that actually holds the operation together is one piece of shared state that everybody can see from wherever they are: the flag burning above the building. It is a single signal, globally visible, that tells every one of them the objective is still live and everyone else is still committed. It is not communication. It is shared state. So the lesson to take is not that splitting up is powerful, which is the lesson the demo version of this teaches. It is narrower and more useful: splitting up works when the split is obvious enough that nobody has to negotiate it, and when there is one piece of shared state that every participant can read. Take away the clean division and you get people duplicating each other's work. Take away the shared signal and you get nine agents improvising against a picture of the world that stopped being true.",
        "narration_ne": "Enies Lobby multi-agent प्रणाली हो, र यो सोच्न उपयोगी छ किनभने यसले दुवै आधा देखाउँछ — ढाँचाले किन काम गर्छ र यो कसरी लगभग भत्किन्छ। संरचना हेर। Crew ढोकामा आइपुग्छ र तुरुन्तै छुट्टिन्छ, र उद्देश्य हासिल नभएसम्म फेरि जम्मा हुँदैन। Zoro ले तरबारबाज लिन्छ। Sanji ले सबैभन्दा छिटो पुग्न सक्ने लिन्छ। Chopper ले चिकित्सकको ज्ञान चाहिने सम्हाल्छ, Nami ले मौसमले लड्ने लिन्छिन्, Usopp ले कसैले उसले लिन्छ भन्ने अपेक्षा नगरेको लिन्छ। नौ जना, एउटै उद्देश्य, र पहिलो मिनेटपछि तिनीबीच लगभग कुनै सञ्चार छैन। त्यो ठ्याक्कै multi-agent प्रणाली हो, र त्यति प्रस्ट भन्दा नै जोखिम कहाँ छन् थाहा हुन्छ। अब, यसले किन काम गर्छ? तिनले उत्कृष्ट समन्वय गरेर होइन, किनभने तिनले समन्वय गर्दै गर्दैनन्। यसले काम गर्छ किनभने कामको बाँडफाँड सफा छ। तरबारबाज कसले लिने भनेर कसैले बैठक गर्नु पर्दैन — काम हरेक जना को हो त्यसैबाट झर्छ। हरेक crew सदस्यसँग साँघुरो, राम्ररी बुझिएको क्षमता छ र त्योसँग मिल्ने उप-समस्या लिन्छ, र समाधान गर्नुपर्ने कुनै अस्पष्टता छैन। त्यो पूर्ण router सहितको supervisor ढाँचा हो: प्रति कार्य एउटा विशेषज्ञ, कडा scope गरिएको, बिनाबहस छानिएको। तिम्रो समस्या त्यति सफासँग टुक्रिन्छ भने, multi-agent साँच्चै सही architecture हो, र यसले मद्दत गर्नुको कारण अघिल्लो slide कै कारण हो — हरेक जना आफूलाई सुहाउने एउटा साँघुरो काम गर्दैछ, समूह आफ्ना सदस्यभन्दा चतुर भएर होइन। अब यो कहाँ लगभग भत्किन्छ हेर, किनभने त्यो बढी उपयोगी आधा हो। कसैलाई अरूले के भेट्यो लगभग थाहा छैन। Robin ले निर्णायक कुरा थाहा पाउँछिन् र अरूलाई भन्ने उपाय छैन। बीस मिनेटअघि सत्य भएको जानकारीमा निर्णय हुन्छन्। अन्तै अवस्था फेरिएकाले पहिल्यै अनावश्यक भइसकेका लडाइँ लडिन्छन्। तीमध्ये हरेक हस्तान्तरणको असफलता हो, र ती कोही गलत भएर होइन — ती कुनै माध्यम नभएर हुन्छन्। र सञ्चालनलाई साँच्चै सँगै राख्ने कुरा एउटा साझा state हो जुन सबैले जहाँबाट पनि देख्न सक्छन्: भवनमाथि बलिरहेको झन्डा। त्यो एउटै सङ्केत हो, विश्वव्यापी रूपमा देखिने, जसले हरेकलाई उद्देश्य अझै जीवित छ र अरू सबै अझै प्रतिबद्ध छन् भन्छ। त्यो सञ्चार होइन। त्यो साझा state हो। त्यसैले लैजाने पाठ छुट्टिनु शक्तिशाली छ भन्ने होइन, जुन यसको demo संस्करणले सिकाउँछ। त्यो साँघुरो र बढी उपयोगी छ: छुट्टिनुले तब काम गर्छ जब बाँडफाँड यति स्पष्ट हुन्छ कि कसैले सौदाबाजी गर्नु पर्दैन, र जब एउटा साझा state हुन्छ जुन हरेक सहभागीले पढ्न सक्छ। सफा बाँडफाँड हटाऊ अनि तिमी एकअर्काको काम दोहोर्‍याउने मानिस पाउँछौ। साझा सङ्केत हटाऊ अनि तिमी सत्य हुन छाडेको संसारको चित्रविरुद्ध सुधार गर्दै हिँडेका नौ agent पाउँछौ।"
      },
      {
        "heading": "The honest default",
        "bullets": [
          "<b>Start with one agent and a good tool set.</b> Add a second only when you can name the failure it fixes and measure that the fix worked. Most systems never need the second.",
          "<b>Prefer a workflow wherever the sequence is knowable.</b> Multi-agent is for genuinely dynamic decomposition — most things called multi-agent in production are pipelines with a model at each stage, and are better for it.",
          "<b>If you split, split cleanly and share one state object.</b> An unambiguous router and one piece of state everyone reads is the difference between Enies Lobby working and nine people improvising.",
          "<b>Instrument before you scale out.</b> Per-agent tracing, token cost and step counts, or you will be unable to tell which specialist is responsible for a bad answer — and you will add a sixth agent to fix it."
        ],
        "narration_en": "Here is the default I would defend, and it is deliberately conservative because the failure mode in this area is enthusiasm. Start with one agent and a good tool set. One model, a well-chosen handful of tools with careful descriptions, a step cap, and good tracing. That configuration handles far more than people expect, and it is cheap, debuggable and explainable. Add a second agent only when you can point at a specific failure it fixes — the prompt has grown so long that instructions are being ignored, or two domains need genuinely incompatible system prompts, or one part of the task needs a much more expensive model than the rest. Those are real reasons. Wanting the architecture to look sophisticated is not, and the resulting system will be worse in every measurable way. Second, prefer a workflow wherever the sequence is knowable, which is the same advice as two lessons ago and it applies with more force here. If you know the steps — research, then draft, then edit — then write that as three function calls with a model in each, and you get something testable, cheap, reproducible and easy to reason about. Calling it a multi-agent system does not change what it is, and structuring it as one adds coordination machinery you do not need. Genuine multi-agent is for the case where the decomposition itself is dynamic: you do not know in advance how many specialists will be needed or which, because that depends on what the first one finds. That case is real and it is rarer than the literature suggests. Third, if you do split, split cleanly and give everyone one shared state object. Ambiguity about which agent owns a decision is the source of most multi-agent pathology — two agents doing the same work, or each assuming the other handled something. Make the routing unambiguous, the way Enies Lobby's was, and make sure there is one thing every participant can read to know the current situation. Passing summaries between agents is lossy; a shared state that each agent updates is not. Fourth, and last: instrument before you scale out, not after. Per-agent tracing, token cost attributed per agent, step counts per run. Without those, a bad final answer is unattributable — you cannot tell which specialist produced it or on what basis — and the instinct when you cannot diagnose a problem is to add another agent to compensate, which makes the next diagnosis harder still. That is how these systems become unmaintainable, and it happens gradually and reasonably at every step. Measure first, then grow.",
        "narration_ne": "यहाँ त्यो पूर्वनिर्धारित छ जुन म बचाउँछु, र यो जानाजान रूढिवादी छ किनभने यस क्षेत्रको असफलताको तरिका उत्साह हो। एउटा agent र राम्रो tool समूहबाट सुरु गर। एउटा model, होसियारीसाथ लेखिएका विवरणसहित राम्ररी छानिएका मुठ्ठीभर tool, पाइला सीमा, र राम्रो tracing। त्यो कन्फिगरेसनले मानिसले सोचेभन्दा धेरै बढी सम्हाल्छ, र यो सस्तो, debug गर्न सकिने र व्याख्या गर्न सकिने छ। दोस्रो agent तब मात्र थप जब तिमी यसले ठीक पार्ने खास असफलता देखाउन सक्छौ — prompt यति लामो भयो कि निर्देशन बेवास्ता हुँदैछन्, वा दुई क्षेत्रलाई साँच्चै नमिल्ने system prompt चाहिन्छ, वा कार्यको एउटा भागलाई बाँकीभन्दा धेरै महँगो model चाहिन्छ। ती साँचा कारण हुन्। Architecture परिष्कृत देखियोस् भन्ने चाहना होइन, र बन्ने प्रणाली हरेक नाप्न सकिने तरिकाले नराम्रो हुनेछ। दोस्रो, क्रम जान्न सकिने जहाँ छ त्यहाँ workflow रुचाऊ, जुन दुई पाठअघिकै सल्लाह हो र यहाँ बढी बलले लागू हुन्छ। तिमीलाई पाइला थाहा छ भने — अनुसन्धान, अनि मस्यौदा, अनि सम्पादन — त्यसलाई हरेकमा एउटा model भएका तीन function call का रूपमा लेख, र तिमी परीक्षणयोग्य, सस्तो, पुनरुत्पादनयोग्य र तर्क गर्न सजिलो कुरा पाउँछौ। यसलाई multi-agent प्रणाली भन्दैमा यो के हो फेरिँदैन, र यसलाई त्यसै संरचना दिँदा तिमीलाई नचाहिने समन्वयको संयन्त्र थपिन्छ। साँचो multi-agent त्यो अवस्थाका लागि हो जहाँ विघटन आफैं गतिशील छ: तिमीलाई कति र कुन विशेषज्ञ चाहिने पहिल्यै थाहा हुँदैन, किनभने त्यो पहिलोले के भेट्छ त्यसमा निर्भर छ। त्यो अवस्था साँचो छ र साहित्यले सुझाएभन्दा दुर्लभ छ। तेस्रो, तिमीले छुट्ट्यायौ भने, सफासँग छुट्ट्याऊ र सबैलाई एउटा साझा state object देऊ। कुन agent कुन निर्णयको मालिक हो भन्ने अस्पष्टता अधिकांश multi-agent रोगको स्रोत हो — दुई agent ले उही काम गर्नु, वा हरेकले अर्कोले सम्हाल्यो होला भन्नु। Enies Lobby को जस्तै routing अस्पष्टतारहित बनाऊ, र हरेक सहभागीले अहिलेको अवस्था जान्न पढ्न सक्ने एउटा कुरा छ भन्ने सुनिश्चित गर। Agent बीच सारांश पठाउनु हानिकारक छ; हरेक agent ले अद्यावधिक गर्ने साझा state होइन। चौथो, र अन्तिम: फैलिनुअघि instrument गर, पछि होइन। प्रति-agent tracing, प्रति agent छुट्याइएको token लागत, प्रति run पाइला गणना। तीबिना, नराम्रो अन्तिम जवाफ कसको हो भन्न सकिँदैन — कुन विशेषज्ञले र कुन आधारमा बनायो तिमी भन्न सक्दैनौ — र समस्या निदान गर्न नसक्दा प्रवृत्ति क्षतिपूर्तिका लागि अर्को agent थप्नु हुन्छ, जसले अर्को निदान झन् गाह्रो बनाउँछ। यी प्रणाली मर्मत गर्न नसकिने यसरी बन्छन्, र यो हरेक पाइलामा क्रमशः र उचित रूपमा हुन्छ। पहिले नाप, अनि बढाऊ।"
      }
    ],
  },

